WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.920
We are now going to try to understand

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.420
a very important concept about circles.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.680
Look at the circle divided into two equal parts.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.120
How do we find the area of this coloured part?

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.040
Let's assume that the radius of the circle is 'R' units.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.940
You are familiar with the area of a circle,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.080
and we can see that it covers half the circle.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.680
So the area will be half multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.120
This is the area of this circle

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.880
and we multiply it by half to find the area of this coloured part.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.740
Now look at this circle which is divided into four equal parts.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.680
What will be the area of this coloured part?

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.260
It will be '1 by 4' multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.460
Since the area of the circle is split into four equal parts,

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.600
we multiply it by 1 by 4.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.100
Let's look at another part of the circle.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.000
This part!

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.100
What will be the area of the coloured region in this case?

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.060
Without any information,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.760
we cannot find the formula for the area of this part.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.420
More importantly,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.380
what we are trying to find is the part of the area of a circle.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.840
These coloured regions are called sectors.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.740
You may think of a sector as a pizza slice.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.980
A sector is covered by two radii and an arc.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.100
In the first case, these are the two radii and this is the arc.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.620
In the second case,

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.700
these two are the radii and this is the arc.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.640
And in the third case,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.900
the sector is covered by these two radii and this arc.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.820
Coming back to the question,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.000
how do we find the area of the sector?

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.780
The area of a sector

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.100
is proportional to the angle that the arc subtends at the center.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.840
It is called the 'sector angle'.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.240
In the first case, this sector angle is '180 degrees'.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.500
Hence, the area of the sector will be '180 by 360'

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.960
multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.100
Out of the total '360 degrees',

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.140
these two radii have '180 degrees' between them.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.020
This value is the same as this value as the ratio equals half.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.840
Similarly, since the sector angle is '90 degrees' in the second case,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.140
the area of this sector will be '90 by 360'

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.360
multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.300
Which equals this.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.200
So if this angle is '30 degrees',

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.320
then the area of the sector will be '30 by 360'

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.480
multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.380
This part is the area of the circle

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.400
and this part tells us how much of the circle is covered.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.400
To generalize the formula for a sector,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.500
we can say that if the sector angle is theta,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.680
then the area of a sector will be 'theta by 360'

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.060
multiplied by 'Pi r squared'.

